@page "/about"

<h1 class="display-4">More about ZIL</h1>
<p class="lead">
    A language made for writing interactive fiction. Developed in the 1970s,
    resurrected in the 2010s, and running in your browser in the 2020s.
</p>

<h3>What is ZIL?</h3>

<p>
    ZIL is the Zork Implementation Language, a programming language developed in the late 1970s by
    <ExternalLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infocom">Infocom</ExternalLink> for writing text adventure games.
    It&rsquo;s a low-level, assembly-like language that&rsquo;s compiled into bytecode for the
    <ExternalLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Z-machine">Z-machine</ExternalLink>, a virtual machine that runs
    ZIL games.
</p>

<p>
    Until 2019, ZIL had been effectively dead. Infocom developed it as their in-house language, and although snippets of
    ZIL code had been published here and there, the full language specification was never released. The only way to
    compile ZIL code was with Infocom&rsquo;s proprietary compiler, which was lost to time when the company shut down.
    Although some intrepid fans had
    <ExternalLink href="https://www.filfre.net/2019/10/new-tricks-for-an-old-z-machine-part-1-digging-the-trenches/">
        reverse-engineered</ExternalLink>
    the virtual machine and written new interpreters, ZIL remained a mystery; the only thing known about it was that it
    was somehow related to <ExternalLink href="https://mdl-language.readthedocs.io/">MDL</ExternalLink>, an language
    developed for AI research in the early 1970s.
</p>

<p>
    In 2019, the Internet Archive&rsquo;s Jason Scott
    <ExternalLink href="https://github.com/historicalsource?tab=repositories">published</ExternalLink> a trove of
    Infocom materials, including the source code to most of Infocom&rsquo;s games. Suddenly, enthusiasts were
    flooded with examples of real-world ZIL code, and the workings of their games could be studied in detail. But
    without a compiler, the code was still just a curiosity.
</p>

<p>
    Luckily, one person had anticipated this moment.
</p>

<h3>What is ZILF?</h3>

<p>
    In 2010, inspired by a
    <ExternalLink
        href="https://waxy.org/2008/04/milliways_infocoms_unreleased_sequel_to_hitchhikers_guide_to_the_galax/">blog
        post</ExternalLink>
    by Andy Baio, Tara McGrew started a project to do the impossible: write a new ZIL compiler from scratch. After much
    research, studying the MDL documentation and every scrap of ZIL code she could find, she had a partially working MDL
    interpreter and a vague idea of how ZIL related to it. The remaining pieces of the puzzle fell into place through a
    series of vivid dreams in which a
    <ExternalLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marc_Summers">TV personality</ExternalLink>
    from her childhood explained the details to her. Thanks to that explanation, and dozens of incredibly lucky guesses,
    she was able to finish a compiler for a language that&mdash;as it turned out&mdash;was a close enough approximation
    of ZIL to compile real games.
</p>

<p>
    The ZILF project is the result of Tara&rsquo;s efforts to bring ZIL back to life. When Infocom&rsquo;s source
    code was published, her compiler was able to compile and run most of it within days.
</p>

<p>
    ZILF consists of:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>A compiler for ZIL (&ldquo;ZILF&rdquo; itself),</li>
    <li>An assembler for Infocom&rsquo;s Z-machine assembly syntax (&ldquo;ZAPF&rdquo;), and</li>
    <li>A library of ZIL code written from scratch that can be used to develop new games (&ldquo;the ZILF
        library&rdquo;, or &ldquo;zillib&rdquo;)</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Because of ZILF&rsquo;s unique relationship to MDL, the compiler also serves as an interpreter for a subset of
    MDL. While it isn&rsquo;t comprehensive enough to run all MDL programs, it is sufficient for running the macros
    that are pervasively used in ZIL code.
</p>

<h3>What is this website?</h3>

<p>
    This website is a playground for experimenting with ZIL, powered by a version of ZILF running in the browser.
    It provides a way to <a href="project">write, compile, and run ZIL code</a> without needing to install any
    software on your computer.  But it&rsquo;s not meant to be a full-featured IDE; for serious development,
    you&rsquo;re better off
    <ExternalLink href="https://foss.heptapod.net/zilf/zilf/-/releases">installing ZILF</ExternalLink>
    and
    <ExternalLink href="https://code.visualstudio.com/">Visual Studio Code</ExternalLink>
    on your own machine.
</p>

<h3>What does ZILF stand for?</h3>

<p>
    ZILF stands for ZIL Forever. It has never stood for anything else. Quit snickering.
</p>

<h3>What does the ZILF logo represent?</h3>

<p>
    It&rsquo;s the cutest, most lovable zombie you&rsquo;ve ever seen! It was drawn by
    <ExternalLink href="https://marquisgraphics.com/">Paul Marquis</ExternalLink> for a completely different project,
    but when Tara saw it, she knew it was perfect for ZILF. After all, ZIL is a dead language that&rsquo;s been
    brought back to life.
</p>

